4.1 The whole estimator
The AUROC is usually drawn as the area under a curve of sensitivity against one-minus-specificity, swept over every threshold. That picture is fine, but it hides how simple the quantity is. The area under the ROC curve is exactly the probability that a randomly chosen event scores higher than a randomly chosen non-event. Stated that way, it is a rank statistic, and it can be computed in one line.
auroc

function (label, score) 
{
    if (length(unique(label)) &lt; 2L) 
        return(NA_real_)
    n1 &lt;- sum(label == 1L)
    n0 &lt;- sum(label == 0L)
    r &lt;- rank(score)
    (sum(r[label == 1L]) - n1 * (n1 + 1)/2)/(n1 * n0)
}
Rank all the scores; sum the ranks belonging to the events; subtract the ranks those events would occupy if they were all crammed at the bottom (\(n_1(n_1+1)/2\)); divide by the number of event/non-event pairs \(n_1 n_0\). What comes out is the fraction of pairs the model orders correctly — the Mann-Whitney U statistic, rescaled. Ties contribute exactly one half, and using average ranks builds that half-credit in with no special case.

5.1 The stratified bootstrap
Chapter 4 produced an estimate of 0.655 in the smallest stratum against a truth of 0.714. The question this chapter answers is whether that discrepancy was detectable — and, more usefully, whether the study could have detected anything at all.
Resample with replacement, recompute, repeat, take percentiles. The only subtlety worth caring about is that resampling is done within outcome class:
boot_ci

function (label, score, fn, n_boot = 2000L, alpha = 0.05, seed = 1L, 
    ...) 
{
    point &lt;- fn(label, score, ...)
    ix1 &lt;- which(label == 1L)
    ix0 &lt;- which(label == 0L)
    if (length(ix1) &lt; 2L || length(ix0) &lt; 2L) {
        return(c(estimate = point, lower = NA_real_, upper = NA_real_))
    }
    set.seed(seed)
    stats &lt;- numeric(n_boot)
    for (b in seq_len(n_boot)) {
        ix &lt;- c(sample(ix1, replace = TRUE), sample(ix0, replace = TRUE))
        stats[b] &lt;- fn(label[ix], score[ix], ...)
    }
    stats &lt;- stats[is.finite(stats)]
    q &lt;- quantile(stats, c(alpha/2, 1 - alpha/2), names = FALSE)
    c(estimate = point, lower = q[1], upper = q[2])
}
&lt;bytecode: 0x114955eb0&gt;
Resampling rows without regard to outcome regularly produces, in a stratum of 39 events, replicates containing no events at all. The metric is then undefined, those replicates get quietly dropped, and the interval becomes a statement about which replicates happened to survive. Stratifying fixes it.

6.1 Two ways to measure it
Chapter 4 established that AUROC cannot see the numbers, only their order. This chapter asks the question it cannot answer: when the model says 0.8, does the event happen 80% of the time?
The calibration slope regresses the outcome on the logit of the predicted probability. Perfect calibration gives slope 1, intercept 0. Slope below 1 means predictions are too extreme; above 1, too conservative.
calibration

function (label, score, eps = 1e-06) 
{
    s &lt;- pmin(pmax(score, eps), 1 - eps)
    fit &lt;- glm(label ~ qlogis(s), family = binomial())
    c(intercept = unname(coef(fit)[1]), slope = unname(coef(fit)[2]))
}
&lt;bytecode: 0x11f3d4ef0&gt;
Expected calibration error bins the predictions and averages the absolute gap between predicted and observed rates within each bin. It is the metric most commonly reported in the machine-learning literature.
ece

function (label, score, n_bins = 10L) 
{
    bins &lt;- cut(score, breaks = seq(0, 1, length.out = n_bins + 
        1L), include.lowest = TRUE)
    parts &lt;- tapply(seq_along(label), bins, function(ix) {
        if (length(ix) == 0L) 
            return(0)
        (length(ix)/length(label)) * abs(mean(label[ix]) - mean(score[ix]))
    })
    sum(unlist(parts), na.rm = TRUE)
}
&lt;bytecode: 0x11fcbca60&gt;
Both are reasonable. On this data they disagree completely.
